WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:05.500
[music]

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.780
Announcer: "We have cleared the tower.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.400
The Cassini spacecraft is on its way to Saturn."

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.300
Barber: "Cassini is nearly out of propellant.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.840
We have 1 percent of the propellant left,

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.100
plus or minus 2 percent.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.020
We can’t risk Cassini becoming a piece of space junk

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.320
around Saturn that could end up crashing into

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.160
one of a few Saturn moons that apparently

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.320
have liquid water environments,

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.120
just a perfect breeding ground for life.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.680
So we cannot risk contaminating those moons

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.880
with earthbound bacterial spores.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.880
We have to preserve those worlds for future exploration.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.860
On September 11, 2017,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:09.460
we’ll do about a 120,000 kilometer flyby of Titan.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.460
That’s close enough that Titan’s gravity

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:12.840
is going to nudge us,

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.320
and that nudge will lower our closest approach to Saturn

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.880
to a point that’s within Saturn’s atmosphere.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:20.880
So from that point forward,

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.880
we only have about half of an orbit.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:26.520
The very last full day of the mission, which is the 14th,

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.020
rather than focus on one target or another,

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.840
we’re giving every scientific discipline

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.360
a chance to say goodbye.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.020
So we have the last Titan picture,

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:38.080
the last Enceladus picture…

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.500
If you’re moving out of a house

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:40.600
you’ve been in for decades,

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.340
you might want to take one last walk

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.840
through the house and say goodbye to each room

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.840
and the memories you had in those rooms,

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:49.840
so that’s what we’re doing."

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.540
[music]

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.440
"So, by the morning of September 15,

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.660
we’ll reconfigure the spacecraft to actually become

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:05.240
a real-time data collector and sender,

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.220
a task for which it was never designed.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:11.760
About 1:37 a.m. Pacific Daylight Time,

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.080
we’ll begin a five-minute roll.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.680
We’ll roll the spacecraft to put INMS,

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.020
the Ion and Neutral Mass Spectrometer,

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:22.680
in a good direction to sniff the atmosphere of Saturn.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.300
A little over three hours later,

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:27.080
we’ll enter the atmosphere,

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.260
and the thrusters will start firing

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.420
to counter balance the torque from the atmosphere.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:34.800
And this is the key moment where we’re

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.460
sniffing Saturn’s upper atmosphere for the first time.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:40.820
The amazing thing is that

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:43.580
out of our twelve science instruments onboard, eight,

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:46.840
everything but the cameras basically, are taking data.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.840
And we hope to take data literally up to the last second.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.340
But at some point the atmosphere wins

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:55.880
and the thrusters even firing 100% of the time

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:57.360
can’t overcome that torque.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.520
So the spacecraft will essentially

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.520
point away from the Earth

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.020
And at that point we can’t maintain high gain data

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:06.800
and get a signal.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.020
From when we first start measuring the atmosphere

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:12.460
until we no longer communicate with the spacecraft,

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.960
that’s essentially one to two minutes.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:17.380
In a way, maybe it’s better that

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:19.160
we don’t see what happens next.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:22.460
We’re screaming in at 76,000 miles per hour.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:24.280
The fact that we’re going so fast makes

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:26.280
a lot of atmospheric friction and heat.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.500
And that heat is what melts

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.320
and then vaporizes the spacecraft.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.320
Essentially, there are different parts of the spacecraft

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.320
that have different melting points.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.320
The thermal blankets will burn off first

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.000
and then aluminum will start to melt.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.260
All those molecules from our earthbound emissary

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:44.900
are then part, permanently, of Saturn’s atmosphere,

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.420
a real ashes to ashes and dust to dust story for Cassini.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.860
[music]

